# §4.19's ladder, rung 5: a window opens, and the library it needs is asked for
# rather than described.
#
#     heroes run examples/raylib/main.hero
#
# **This program is the acceptance test for `package`.** raylib is the ladder's
# first *third-party* library — rungs 1 to 4 are all system libraries — and it is
# the rung that was unclimbable for a milestone. Three panels went looking for
# why. The answer was not the one anybody expected: not the missing `-framework`
# spelling, but the missing **search path**. On this machine the header is under
# `/opt/homebrew/include` and the library under `/opt/homebrew/lib`, and the
# language had no way to say either.
#
# `package "raylib"` says both, and says them the same way everywhere: the
# machine is asked, and it answers with `-I`, `-L` and `-lraylib` here, with
# `-lGL -lX11 -lm -ldl` on Linux, and with frameworks where frameworks are what
# is needed. One line, no platform names in the program — which is what panel 049
# refused a platform axis in favour of.
#
# What it does NOT prove, and the record should not be read as claiming it does:
# rung 5's text also promises *"real struct passing"*, and **383 of raylib's 600
# entry points pass or return a struct by value**. `ClearBackground(Color)` does
# not compile, because Heroes has seven boundary types and none of them is a
# struct. This program uses `DrawFPS`, which is the one draw call that takes only
# integers. The type vocabulary is the next wall and it is untouched.
#
# **It does not open a window, and that is the second thing this file learned.**
# The first version did, and it passed here and failed on the macOS CI leg —
# because a runner has no display, and raylib cannot initialise one. A corpus
# program runs unattended in three configurations on a headless machine, so the
# binding it exercises must be one that needs no screen. `SetRandomSeed` and
# `GetRandomValue` are real raylib entry points that touch no window, and a seed
# makes the answer the same on every run and every platform.
#
# The window *was* opened, by hand, on 2026-08-14, from this same
# `package "raylib"` line — `InitWindow`/`CloseWindow`, printing
# "the window opened and closed". That is the rung, and it is recorded in
# `docs/panel/050` rather than asserted by a test no runner can run.

extern "raylib.h" package "raylib"
    function SetRandomSeed(seed: u32)
    function GetRandomValue(min: i32, max: i32) -> i32

function main()
    SetRandomSeed(7)
    print("raylib answered ", GetRandomValue(min: 1, max: 6))

test "the binding reaches raylib and comes back with a value in range"
    SetRandomSeed(1)
    roll = GetRandomValue(min: 1, max: 6)
    assert roll >= 1
    assert roll <= 6
